While a writing suggestion is showing, a plain Tab in Ladybird accepts
it before the page sees the key. The page gets a keydown with the key
Tab and the keyCode 229, and canceling it doesn’t stop the insertion.

WebKit does the same: WebViewImpl::interpretKeyEvent() hands the key to
NSTextInputContext’s “handleEventByInputMethod:” first, and AppKit
takes Tab to accept the inline prediction. WebCore’s EventHandler::
internalKeyEvent() then sets the keydown’s keyCode to 229, its
CompositionEventKeyCode, skips the keydown’s default handling, and
dispatches no keypress. WebPage::handleEditingKeyboardEvent() inserts
the text after the keydown.

Neither Blink nor Gecko shows macOS inline predictions in web content.
